---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import EngineName from "../../components/EngineName.astro";
import AnthusNote from "../../components/AnthusNote.astro";
import { models, modelById, urls, OWA, CWA, TASK_INFO, taskBySlug, pct, pts, fmt, int, overall, axisRow, pairRow, retest, latency, isComplete, L, listOf } from "../../lib/site.js";
import { intro } from "../../lib/content.js";
import { fullModels, lean, deepEnd } from "../../lib/insights.js";
import { tally, predictions } from "../../lib/predictions.js";

const full = fullModels();
const owaDone = models.filter((m) => isComplete(m.id, OWA));
const LUNA = "openai-gpt-6-luna-effort-none";
const jev = modelById.jev;
const jl0 = pairRow(OWA, "jev", LUNA, "depth", "0"), jl5 = pairRow(OWA, "jev", LUNA, "depth", "5");
const rt = retest("jev", OWA);
const t = tally();
const failed = predictions().flatMap((sec) => sec.predictions.filter((p) => p.status === "failed").map((p) => `“${p.text.split(/(?<=\.)\s/)[0]}”`));
const jd = jev && isComplete("jev", OWA) ? [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map((d) => axisRow("jev", OWA, "depth", String(d))) : [];
const jmin = jd.length ? jd.reduce((a, b) => (b.accuracy < a.accuracy ? b : a)) : null;
---
<Base title={intro.evaluating.title} description={intro.evaluating.intro} path={urls.page("evaluating-decision-models")} current={{ page: "evaluating" }}
  crumbs={[{ label: "Benchmark", href: urls.home() }, { label: "Evaluating decision models" }]}>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <p class="kicker">Guide · evaluating decision models</p>
    <h1 class="display xl">{intro.evaluating.title}</h1>
    <p class="deck">{intro.evaluating.intro}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section tight" aria-labelledby="d5-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="d5-h">1. Score by difficulty, not with one number</h2>
    <p class="lede">Averages hide where a model stops working. On the open-world task, every model's accuracy falls between depth 0 (the answer is stated) and depth 5 (five chained inferences):</p>
    <ul class="around-list">
      {owaDone.map((m) => { const d = deepEnd(m.id, OWA); return (
        <li><EngineName engine={m} href={urls.model(m.id)} /><span>{pct(d.d0.accuracy)}% at depth 0, {pct(d.d5.accuracy)}% at depth 5{d.atChance ? `; within reach of chance from depth ${d.atChance.value}` : ""}</span><span class="av">{pct(d.d5.accuracy)}%</span></li>
      ); })}
    </ul>
    {jl0 && jl5 && <p>Two models can look close on easy items and far apart on hard ones. Jev and GPT-6 Luna (reasoning off) are {pts(jl0.diff)} points apart at depth 0 and {pts(jl5.diff)} points apart at depth 5 on the same open-world problems. If your decisions need several steps of reasoning, the easy-item number is the wrong one to choose on. <a href={urls.axis("depth")}>Accuracy by proof depth</a>.</p>}
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="cls-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="cls-h">2. Check every answer, especially "unknown"</h2>
    <p class="lede">A model can reach a respectable average by ignoring one answer entirely. On the open-world task a third of the correct answers are unknown:</p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="cell-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Model</th>{taskBySlug[OWA].options.map((o) => <th scope="col" class="num">Recall: {o}</th>)}<th scope="col">Most common answer</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{owaDone.map((m) => { const o = overall(m.id, OWA); const l = lean(m.id, OWA); return (
          <tr><th scope="row"><EngineName engine={m} href={urls.model(m.id)} /></th>{taskBySlug[OWA].options.map((x) => <td class="num">{pct(o.recall[x])}%</td>)}<td>{l.answer}, {pct(l.share)}% of answers (correct on {pct(l.gold)}% of items)</td></tr>
        ); })}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Recall on each answer and the answer mix take one look and catch a model that has learned a default rather than the task. <a href={urls.axis("reference_label")}>True, false and unknown</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="floor-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="floor-h">3. Read every number against a floor</h2>
    <p>Chance is a third on the open-world task and a half on the closed-world one. A stricter floor is the best constant guess: always giving the most common answer in that slice. {owaDone.map((m) => { const o = overall(m.id, OWA); return `${m.label} is ${pts(o.accuracy - o.best_constant)} points above it`; }).join("; ")} (open world). A model that is only a few points above the constant guess is mostly not using the rules.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="pair-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="pair-h">4. Compare models on the same items</h2>
    <p>Every model here answered the same problems, so differences come with a paired interval that is narrower than two separate ones. Where two models are close, only a paired comparison can say whether the gap is real. <a href={urls.models()}>Paired comparisons</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="rt-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="rt-h">5. Ask twice</h2>
    <p>{rt ? `Asked every open-world question a second time, Jev gave the same answer on ${pct(rt.agreement)}% of items; ${rt.changed} answers changed, mostly on deeper proofs.` : "Reruns are in progress."} A decision that changes when you ask again is hard to audit and hard to threshold. Measure it on your own decisions, and check calibration if the model returns probabilities. <a href={urls.page("repeatability")}>Consistency and calibration</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="lat-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="lat-h">6. Time it where you will run it</h2>
    <p>Latency depends on where the model runs as much as on the model. Our hosted numbers include the network; our open-model numbers are one laptop. Neither predicts your deployment. <a href={urls.speed()}>Latency</a> · <a href={urls.speed()}>Sizes and memory</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="pre-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="pre-h">7. Write down what you expect first</h2>
    <p>We preregistered our predictions before any model answered. So far {t.held || 0} held and {t.failed || 0} failed{t.mixed || t["partly held"] ? `, with ${(t.mixed || 0) + (t["partly held"] || 0)} mixed or partly held` : ""}. {failed.length > 0 && `The failures were the informative part: ${listOf(failed)}. `}An evaluation that only confirms what you expected is easy to fool yourself with. <a href={urls.predictions()}>Every prediction</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="jev-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="jev-h">Evaluating Jev: what this benchmark shows</h2>
    {jev && isComplete("jev", OWA) && (
      <p>Jev scored {pct(overall("jev", OWA).accuracy)}% on the open-world task and {pct(overall("jev", CWA).accuracy)}% on the closed-world one, the highest of the models tested. Its weakest answer is unknown ({pct(overall("jev", OWA).recall.unknown)}% recall against {pct(overall("jev", OWA).recall.true)}% on true); its accuracy falls from {pct(jd[0].accuracy)}% at depth 0 to its lowest, {pct(jmin.accuracy)}%, at depth {jmin.value}{jmin.value !== "5" ? `, and is ${pct(jd[5].accuracy)}% at depth 5` : ""}. Paraphrased rules cost it {pts(axisRow("jev", OWA, "paraphrased", "False").accuracy - axisRow("jev", OWA, "paraphrased", "True").accuracy)} points. Those are the places to test first on your own data. <a href={urls.model("jev")}>Jev's full results</a>.</p>
    )}
  </section>

  <AnthusNote lead="This benchmark measures decision models along these lines: the decision's own difficulty axis, every answer class, paired comparisons, repeatability and predictions written down first. The same measurements apply to your own decisions." />
</Base>
